# Push & local notifications

**Primary source:** Firebase Cloud Messaging (FCM) — Google's own push
notification service for Android, and the `NotificationManagerCompat`/
`NotificationChannel` APIs for displaying notifications. Firebase is not
part of AOSP, but it is unambiguously Google's own product — treat FCM as
the primary source, the same way `push-notifications.mdc` treats APNs as
Apple's own on iOS.

## Registration

- Request the runtime **`POST_NOTIFICATIONS`** permission (Android 13+)
  **at a contextually relevant moment** in the product flow, not
  immediately on first launch — this is both good practice and a
  `privacy-permissions.mdc` concern.
- Retrieve the FCM registration token
  (`FirebaseMessaging.getInstance().token`) and forward it to a `core`
  service that registers it with the backend through the standard
  `ApiClient` (`networking-api-client.mdc`) — not a bespoke networking
  path.
- Handle token **rotation** (`FirebaseMessagingService.onNewToken`) by
  re-registering with the backend — a token is not stable for the
  lifetime of an install.

## Notification channels

- Declare `NotificationChannel`s explicitly at app startup (required on
  Android 8+) grouped by purpose (e.g. "system-alerts", "account") —
  not one generic channel for everything, which removes the user's
  ability to control categories independently in system settings.

## Handling incoming messages

- Implement `FirebaseMessagingService.onMessageReceived` in one place (a
  `core` service, not scattered across features).
- **Data messages** (silent push) trigger a `WorkManager` request
  (`app-lifecycle-background.mdc`) to refresh data through the standard
  `ApiClient`/`AppDatabase` path — they do not bypass the app's normal
  data-loading services.
- **Notification tap** resolves to the same typed `AppRoute`
  (`navigation-deep-linking.mdc`) used by App Links and App Shortcuts —
  do not build a separate navigation path just for
  notification-originated deep links.

## Local notifications

- Local notifications (reminders, timers) go through the same
  `NotificationManagerCompat` API as remote ones; do not introduce a
  separate local-notification abstraction.

## Testing

- Token-registration and message-routing logic (message payload →
  `AppRoute`/`WorkRequest`) are unit-testable by constructing the
  relevant data objects directly — no real FCM infrastructure needed for
  this.
- End-to-end delivery (an actual push arriving) is verified manually per
  release, documented in the feature map — this is not part of the
  automated CI suite.

## Anti-patterns

- **Requesting notification permission on first launch** before the user
  has any context for why the app wants to notify them.
- **One generic notification channel** for all notification types.
- **A second, bespoke deep-link resolver** for notification taps instead
  of reusing the shared `AppRoute` resolution from
  `navigation-deep-linking.mdc`.

## Related rules

- **Deep linking:** `navigation-deep-linking.mdc`
- **Background execution (WorkManager on data message):** `app-lifecycle-background.mdc`
- **Privacy/permission prompts:** `privacy-permissions.mdc`
- **Networking:** `networking-api-client.mdc`
